WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.600
Imagine you're on a beach.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.180
It's flat, it's wide. With pristine sand.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.260
Looks nice, right?

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.280
Unfortunately, many beaches don't look this
way.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.040
They’re narrow, with steep cliffs, and waves
breaking close to the property line.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.340
This is a beach that’s experiencing erosion.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.720
In America, about 80 to 90 percent of sandy coastlines

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.699
have this problem.

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:35.140
So the government spends billions to expand
some of the most rapidly eroding beaches in an

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.240
effort to defend the coast.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:42.220
But this effort, while effective in the short
term, can actually hurt beaches in the long run.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.840
It's because every shoreline on the planet is subject to erosion.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.060
Beach erosion occurs when waves and
currents remove sand from the shoreline.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.880
The loss of sand makes the beach narrower and
lowers its elevation.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:05.180
This erosion becomes a problem when it reaches
structures built by humans along the coast.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.080
Especially for beaches that generate tourism.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.760
The visitors enjoy the sandy coasts while
the cities and towns nearby enjoy the revenue gained.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.940
But the driving factor there is the beach
— a place like Miami Beach wouldn’t have

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.030
the same draw if there weren't lots of sand.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.640
In fact there was a time when it didn’t
look this way at all.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.040
In the 1970s, a seawall turned

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.140
the beach in Miami into a narrow strip.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.060
But by the '80s, the beach in Miami re-emerged

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.080
nice and wide.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.160
How?

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.520
Well, coastal engineers
rebuilt it through a process called "beach nourishment."

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:48.900
Beach nourishment is a shore protection strategy
to try to counter the loss, the natural loss of sand.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.310
The typical way to do this is with dredging.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.820
Boats will dig up sand from a borrow site and move it onto the beach.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.630
You'll have a big pipe pump and you'll suck
up the sand.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.990
Then it's transferred to the coastline.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:08.300
Where it's dumped or pumped out onto the beach

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.780
and then bulldozers move it around to try

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:17.080
to mimic what the natural beach was like before
the project took place.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.300
The result is a nice wide beach.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.540
The new profile will better defend the property
line from damage during more intense weather

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.900
like storm surge flooding.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.900
In the United States, beach nourishment is the main strategy

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.440
used to protect coastal properties from risky

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:33.980
erosion.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:35.860
But there’s a problem.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:37.940
The protection doesn’t last.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.120
As the constant
beating of waves and wind takes the sand away

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.130
from the shore.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.930
And it soon it looks like it did before the nourishment
occurred.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.910
Every 2 to 8 years, on average, the nourishments
need to be repeated.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:51.520
Like this beach in Florida.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.790
Well this morning, Lido Beach is under a local state
of emergency —

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.030
Just look at how powerful the wind was earlier
today —

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.260
— problem getting worse by the hour.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.100
Lido Beach, on the Gulf Coast of Florida, got an emergency nourishment

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.599
in 2018 after damage from storms reduced the

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.140
beach to a narrow strip.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.160
But the beach had already gotten new sand
15 times since 1964.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.680
And Lido Key isn’t an outlier.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:22.349
More than 200 of the 400 miles of critically
eroding coastlines in Florida have received

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:24.430
one or more nourishments.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:30.120
And across the United States, there have been nearly
3000 known-nourishment events since 1923.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:35.920
The funding for these projects gets a little
wonky, but here's what's important: The federal

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.510
government pays for a lot of these nourishments.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.440
Up to 65% of the cost.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.459
State and local funds will make up the rest.

00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:47.190
But not all beaches that want or need nourishment
will get it.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.590
The Army Corps of Engineers — the group that
approves and designs nourishments —prioritizes

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:56.280
defending some beaches over others, based on
the potential loss of value.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.930
According to ProPublica, the Corps only funds
nourishments where the expected benefit is

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.240
2 and a half times as high as the cost.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.300
Poorer communities won’t often meet that
criteria.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.640
So places like Miami Beach, Florida, and Ocean City, Maryland,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.980
are more likely to get a lot of nourishment.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.940
They have the expensive shorefront developments
that make the investment worthwhile.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:21.459
And for beaches that don’t make the cut
for nourishment, continued erosion can lead

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.580
to damaged or destroyed property.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:28.740
Nourishments aren't just about protecting
buildings, but also protecting the economies

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.280
tied to them and the beach.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:37.120
Consider the 200 million dollars spent on
nourishments in Florida from 1995-2001.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:43.640
That might seem like a lot of money, until you see the
revenue from coastal tourism — it was $21.6 billion

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.440
in just one year — 2001.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:51.400
On average, the State of Florida generates
more than 5 dollars of revenue for every dollar

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.840
invested in beach nourishment.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.380
Which is why nourishment is so appealing.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:58.100
It make economic sense.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.860
But they do present one major problem.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.080
According to research published by the American
Geophysical Union, there is a feedback loop.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.940
Nourishment tends to happen along beaches
that generally have expansive properties and

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.280
they also seem to drive development along
the same shores, despite the risk of future

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:17.280
erosion.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.250
If you were in a place that had nourished
its beach, the houses behind that nourishment

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:28.850
project were significantly larger, in every
case, than in a place that had never nourished

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:30.280
its shoreline at all.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:36.080
Research found that areas with nourished beaches
had homes that were about three times bigger

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.520
than non-nourished ones.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:43.040
And this excessive development is a real problem,
because it's based on false security.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.740
According to the researchers, "beach nourishment
may actually mask or reduce the apparent impact

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:52.060
of coastal hazards without changing the natural
processes that drive them."

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.840
In fact, building more property in these areas

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.460
only increases the potential damage from future erosion.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:05.051
So, while beach nourishments protect property
and local economies in the short run, they

00:06:05.051 --> 00:06:08.750
also trick us into thinking it’s safe to
build in places that aren't.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:14.000
Which sets up coastal communities for an ugly
reckoning at the shore... sooner or later.

